+# Reads Keyed by a Caller-Supplied Value Stay Out of the Vet Cache
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+Every read method on `VetRepository` is cached in the single `vets` cache. The cache is declared through the JCache API with statistics only: no size limit, no expiry, no eviction. The specialty filter adds a read whose key includes a free string from the request address. Caching it would let any caller add one cache entry per distinct string, with nothing ever removing it.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Cache the narrowed read like the others.** Unbounded growth from caller-chosen keys: a memory-exhaustion path open to any client, since no route is authenticated.
+2. **Cache it under a normalized key and bound the cache.** Needs a size limit, which the JCache API cannot express. Setting one means vendor-specific configuration for a read over a handful of rows.
+3. **Leave the narrowed read uncached** (chosen). Each narrowed request queries the database once. The unfiltered reads keep their cache unchanged.
+
+## Decision
+
+A repository read whose arguments include a value the caller chooses freely is not cached. Page numbers on the unfiltered directory remain cached, as today. A later read that needs caching first bounds the cache, recorded in a new decision.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Cache memory stays bounded by the unfiltered reads alone, whatever callers send.
+- Narrowed requests cost one database round trip each; at the recorded directory size this is negligible.
+- Narrowed results never go stale against out-of-band vet edits, unlike the cached unfiltered list (system-design Open Question 5).
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Requirements:** REQ-VET-003
+
+## References
+
+- [system-design.md § Scale and Load](../system-design.md#scale-and-load)
+- [system-design.md § Threat Model](../system-design.md#threat-model)
