+# Case-Insensitive Matching Is Expressed in the Query, Not the Schema
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+The veterinarian directory gains a filter by specialty name that must ignore letter case on H2, MySQL, and PostgreSQL alike. The three vendor schemas store text differently. MySQL compares case-insensitively under its default collation; H2 `VARCHAR` and PostgreSQL `TEXT` compare case-sensitively. Owner search already relies on the schema for case handling, and the PostgreSQL schema breaks it — a recorded known defect against REQ-OWN-002.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Rely on column collation or type per vendor.** Change the H2 and PostgreSQL schemas so the specialty name compares case-insensitively. Correctness then depends on three hand-maintained scripts agreeing, which is the failure owner search shows.
+2. **Lower-case both sides in the query** (chosen). The query compares `lower(name)` to `lower(parameter)`. Behavior is identical on every vendor, and no schema changes.
+3. **Lower-case in Java, store lower-cased names.** Requires a data migration and a write-path rule the application has no write path to enforce.
+
+## Decision
+
+A case-insensitive match is written in the query itself, applying the same lower-casing function to both the stored value and the parameter. Matching is equality on the whole value, never `LIKE`, so no wildcard character in the parameter carries meaning. A new case-insensitive lookup follows the same form; it does not depend on vendor collation.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The specialty filter behaves the same on all three databases without a schema edit.
+- A function over the column defeats the plain name index. The specialty table is small, so the cost is accepted (see system-design § Scale and Load).
+- Owner search still depends on the schema and keeps its PostgreSQL defect; repairing it is a separate slice.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Requirements:** REQ-VET-003
+
+## References
+
+- [system-design.md § Persistence](../system-design.md#persistence)
+- [system-design.md § Known Defects](../system-design.md#known-defects)
